course: geography
lesson: 22
title: Winds, monsoons and ocean currents
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: On a sunny afternoon at the beach the land heats faster than the sea, the warm air over the sand rises, and cooler air slides in from the water to take its place: that is a sea breeze. Wind everywhere is the same thing, air rushing from where the pressure is high to where it is low.
explain: On the scale of the whole planet this makes belts of steady winds: the trade winds blowing from the east in the tropics, the westerlies over places like Britain, calm patches at the equator, and wild westerlies far in the south.
explain: Because Earth spins, moving air and water do not go straight but curve, to the right in the north and to the left in the south. That curving turns ocean currents into giant wheels called gyres.
explain: Over India the winds swap with the seasons: in summer wet wind blows in from the ocean and brings the monsoon rains, in winter dry wind blows out from the land.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 the Indian Ocean trade between Gujarat, Calicut, Aden and Malacca flows all year round. Real ships could not do that: they sailed with the monsoon and often waited months in port for the wind to turn, and the way out and the way back across the Atlantic were different routes.
fact: Wind is air moving from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure.
fact: The trade winds blow steadily from the east towards the equator in the tropics, from the north-east in the northern hemisphere and the south-east in the southern.
fact: In the middle latitudes, such as over Britain, the main winds blow from the west and are called the westerlies.
fact: Because Earth spins, moving air and water are bent to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern hemisphere, which is called the Coriolis effect.
fact: The Coriolis effect is too weak to decide which way water drains from a bath or a sink.
fact: The South Asian monsoon is a seasonal reversal of winds: in summer moist winds blow from the Indian Ocean onto India, and in winter dry winds blow out from the land.
fact: Arab, Persian, Indian and Chinese sailors timed their voyages across the Indian Ocean to the monsoon, often waiting months in port for the wind to turn.
fact: The Gulf Stream is a strong, warm ocean current that flows from the Gulf of Mexico up the east coast of North America and then across the Atlantic.
fact: Each big ocean has huge circular currents called gyres, turning clockwise in the northern hemisphere and anticlockwise in the southern, and floating rubbish such as plastic gets trapped in their calm middle.
fact: Cold currents such as the Humboldt Current off Peru and the Benguela Current off south-west Africa flow towards the equator along the western coasts of continents; they bring plenty of food for fish but little rain, so the shores beside them are often desert.
fact: Near the equator lies a zone of weak, changing winds called the doldrums, where sailing ships could be stuck for days.
fact: Between about 40 and 50 degrees south, strong westerly winds blow almost without land to slow them down, and sailors called them the Roaring Forties; Dutch ships used them to race east from the Cape of Good Hope towards the East Indies.
fact: Columbus sailed south to the Canary Islands first so that the north-east trade winds could carry him across the Atlantic; ships came home further north, with the westerlies behind them.
exercise: What is wind? | Air moving from high pressure to low pressure | Air moving from low pressure to high pressure | Air pushed along by the spinning Moon | Air blown out of clouds by rain
exercise: From which direction do the trade winds blow in the northern hemisphere? | From the north-east | From the south-west | From the north-west | Straight from the north
exercise: From which direction do the main winds over Britain usually blow? | From the west | From the east | From the north | From the south
exercise: Which way does Earth's spin bend moving air in the northern hemisphere? | To the right | To the left side | Straight upwards | It does not bend it at all
exercise: Does the Coriolis effect decide which way a sink drains? | No, it is far too weak for that | Yes, sinks drain the opposite way in Australia | Yes, but only on the equator | Yes, in every country in the world
exercise: What happens to the winds over India in the monsoon year? | Wet sea winds in summer, dry land winds in winter | Dry sea winds in summer, wet land winds in winter | The wind blows from the north all year | There is no wind at all in summer
exercise: Why did Indian Ocean sailors often wait months in port? | They had to wait for the monsoon wind to turn | Ports charged them to leave before winter | Their ships needed new sails every month | They waited for the sea to freeze over
exercise: Where does the Gulf Stream begin? | In the Gulf of Mexico | In the Arctic Ocean | Off the coast of Peru | In the Mediterranean Sea
exercise: Is the Gulf Stream warm or cold? | Warm | Cold | Frozen on top | Warm in winter and cold in summer
exercise: What is a gyre? | A huge circle of ocean currents | A storm that spins round over land | A deep trench on the ocean floor | A wave that crosses a whole ocean
exercise: Which way do gyres turn in the northern hemisphere? | Clockwise | Anticlockwise | They change every season | They do not turn at all
exercise: Which current flows cold along the coast of Peru? | The Humboldt Current | The Gulf Stream | The Benguela Current | The Roaring Forties
exercise: Where do cold currents usually flow? | Along the western coasts of continents, towards the equator | Along the eastern coasts of continents, away from the equator to the poles | Only around the North Pole, under the ice | Only in the middle of the oceans, far from land
exercise: What are the doldrums? | A zone of weak, changing winds near the equator | A belt of storms around the South Pole | A warm current flowing past Britain | Strong dry winds blowing over the Sahara
exercise: Where do the Roaring Forties blow? | Between about 40 and 50 degrees south | Between about 40 and 50 degrees north | Right along the equator | Around the Arctic Circle
exercise: Why did Columbus go south to the Canaries before heading west? | To catch the north-east trade winds | To pick up the Gulf Stream there | To avoid the Roaring Forties | Spain had no western ports
test: Why does a beach often get a breeze from the sea in the afternoon? | The land heats faster, its air rises and sea air flows in | The sea is warmer than the land in the afternoon | The tide pushes the air towards the land | The Sun pulls the air off the water
test: My cousin swears the toilet flushes backwards in Australia cos of the Earth spinning. True? | No, the Coriolis effect is far too weak for a toilet | Yes, everything drains the other way round down there | Yes, but only at night, when the Earth spins faster | No, because Australian toilets do not use water
test: In the game, a fleet sails from Calicut to Aden any month you like. What did real ships do? | They sailed with the monsoon and often waited months for it | They also sailed whenever they liked, all year | They only sailed in winter, when the sea froze | They rowed the whole way, so wind did not matter
test: Ships came home from the Americas to Spain by a more northern route than they went out. Why? | Going out they used the trade winds, coming back the westerlies | The southern route was blocked by sea ice on the way back to Europe | Sailors wanted to stop at different islands on the way home | The ships were lighter going home, so they could go faster up north
test: Off Peru the fishing is some of the richest in the world, yet the shore is desert. Why? | The cold Humboldt Current brings food for fish but little rain | The hot desert sand blows into the sea and feeds the fish there | The fishing boats' smoke scares the rain clouds away from the land | Warm water there makes it rain out at sea but never over the shore
test: ugh we've been stuck in the doldrums for a week, no wind. where are we prob | Close to the equator | Near the South Pole itself | In the Roaring Forties | Off the coast of Britain
test: What would happen to sailing ships if the trade winds stopped blowing? | Crossing the tropical Atlantic would take far longer | Ships would sail much faster westwards across the ocean | The Gulf Stream would turn cold at once and stop | The monsoon would blow all year round instead
test: Why does floating plastic collect in the middle of the North Pacific? | The gyre's currents circle around and trap it in the centre | The plastic sinks and then rises in one spot | Ships dump all their rubbish in one place on purpose | The wind always blows towards the middle of every ocean
test: Dutch ships raced from the Cape of Good Hope to the East Indies far in the south. Why there? | The Roaring Forties blew them east very fast | The doldrums were calm and safe there | The trade winds blow eastwards near the poles | The water there was warmer and smoother
test: Ben says the wind blows from low pressure to high pressure. Right? | No, it blows from high pressure to low pressure | Yes, air moves to where there is more air | Yes, but only at sea | No, wind has nothing to do with pressure
test: Big storms north of the equator spin anticlockwise and south of it clockwise. What causes the difference? | Earth's spin bends moving air different ways in each half | The Moon pulls storms in opposite directions in each half of the world | The water is much saltier south of the equator | The Sun shines on the two halves from opposite sides
test: If you sailed with a gyre in the North Atlantic, which way would you be carried round? | Clockwise | Anticlockwise | Straight to the North Pole | Back and forth on the spot
exam: Identify the principal reason for which mariners trading across the Indian Ocean customarily remained in port for several months. | Voyages were contingent upon the seasonal reversal of the monsoon winds | Port authorities prohibited all departures during the summer months by law | The ocean was rendered impassable by ice during winter | Vessels required wholesale reconstruction after every voyage
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the trade winds? | Steady winds blowing from the east towards the equator within the tropics | Variable winds blowing from the west over the middle latitudes of both hemispheres | Gales prevailing between forty and fifty degrees south | Calm and inconstant airs confined to the equatorial belt
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the deflection of moving air by the rotation of the Earth. | Air is deflected to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern | Air is deflected to the left in the northern hemisphere and to the right in the southern | Air is deflected solely in the vicinity of the equator | Such deflection determines the direction of drainage in domestic basins
exam: It is given that a current of cold water flows towards the equator along the western coast of a continent. Ascertain which current this description fits. | The Benguela Current off south-western Africa | The Gulf Stream off eastern North America | The North Atlantic Drift off western Europe | The current of the Roaring Forties off Antarctica
